/* ==============================================================
   05 — Turn configuration: the last joint
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   SetConfTurn, SetAutoConfTurn, and joint 6
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, no tool mounted
   WATCH     Joint 6 reaching the same angle by two routes
   TIME      ~30 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   JOINT 6 IS DIFFERENT

   The first five joints have mechanical limits. Joint 6 does
   not — it can spin, and its software limit is plus or minus 100
   turns.

   That creates an ambiguity the other joints do not have. If you
   ask for a tool orientation, joint 6 at 10 degrees and joint 6
   at 370 degrees produce exactly the same orientation. The tool
   looks identical. The robot has turned a full extra revolution.

   The TURN CONFIGURATION parameter, ct, says which revolution
   you meant:

       SetConfTurn(ct)      ct an integer from -100 to 100

   It selects the range for joint 6 as
   -180 + ct*360 < theta6 <= 180 + ct*360. So ct = 0 is the
   normal range, ct = 1 is one full turn further on, and so on.

   WHY ANYONE CARES: CABLES

   For a bare tool, none of this matters and you should leave it
   alone. It matters when something is CABLED to the end of the
   arm — a powered gripper, a camera, an air line, a sensor.

   Cable routing at 5 degrees and at 365 degrees is not the same
   thing at all. One is slack; the other has a full wrap in it.
   Do that a few thousand times in one direction and the cable
   fails. Cable failure at the wrist is one of the most common
   causes of unplanned downtime on any robot, and it is entirely
   avoidable.

   The recommended treatment for a cabled tool:
     1. Restrict joint 6 with SetJointLimits to the range the
        cable can actually tolerate.
     2. Specify the turn with SetConfTurn so the robot always
        arrives at a pose the same way round.

   For a cable-less tool, leave automatic turn selection on. The
   manual is explicit about this: with no cabling, automatic turn
   configuration should never be disabled.

   ONE PRACTICAL WARNING

   Before powering down, bring joint 6 back within plus or minus
   420 degrees. Otherwise the robot may not know which revolution
   it woke up in.

   BY DEFAULT

       SetAutoConfTurn(1)   robot chooses the nearest (default)
       SetAutoConfTurn(0)   freeze the current turn
       SetConfTurn(ct)      demand a specific revolution
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Seeing the ambiguity directly -----------------------------
   These two joint sets are the same robot POSTURE. Joint 6 ends
   up pointing the same way both times. But getting from one to
   the other is a full revolution of the wrist — which you can
   watch happen.

   Note that we use MoveJoints here, because joint angles are the
   only way to state "the same orientation, one turn further on"
   unambiguously. */
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 30)
Delay(1.5)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 390)      // 30 + 360: same orientation
Delay(1.5)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 30)       // and back
Delay(1.5)


/* --- Controlling it from a Cartesian pose ----------------------
   Now the same idea through MovePose, where you specify an
   orientation and the robot decides which revolution to use. */

MovePose(190, 0, 280, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)

// Demand the normal range: joint 6 lands between -180 and 180.
SetConfTurn(0)
MovePose(190, 0, 280, 0, 90, 90)
Delay(1.5)

// Demand one turn further on. Identical pose command, identical
// tool orientation at the end — and the wrist takes a whole
// extra revolution to get there. Watch joint 6, not the tip.
SetConfTurn(1)
MovePose(190, 0, 280, 0, 90, 90)
Delay(1.5)

// Back to the normal range.
SetConfTurn(0)
MovePose(190, 0, 280, 0, 90, 0)


// --- Hand control back and go home ----------------------------
// For a cable-less tool this is the correct state to leave in.
SetAutoConfTurn(1)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. This one only sells to people with cabled end-effectors —
      but to those people it sells hard. Ask whether they have
      ever replaced a wrist cable. The answer is usually yes, and
      usually with feeling.

   2. Run the two SetConfTurn poses back to back. Same command,
      same final orientation, one takes a full extra turn. Then
      point out that without SetConfTurn, which one you get is
      the robot's choice, and it can differ between cycles.

   NEXT   06_blending.mxprog — cycle time you get for free.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
